/**
  @page examples-swift-background Adapting and fitting an implicit interface
  @brief Prepare a graded background with MMG, classify a circle, and fit its interface with SWIFT.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-swift-background-question Geometry and workflow

  Consider a circle of radius @f$R=0.25@f$ centered at
  @f$c=(0.5,0.5)@f$ inside the unit square. Its signed distance and gradient are
  @f[
    \phi(x)=\lVert x-c\rVert-R,
    \qquad \nabla\phi(x)=\frac{x-c}{\lVert x-c\rVert}.
  @f]
  The negative phase is the disk. The gradient is undefined at the center;
  the callable returns zero there, away from the interface being fitted.

  A background mesh must provide enough resolution near the circle and a
  set of facets that can represent its boundary. The example uses three stages:

  1. MMG changes the background mesh according to a positive size field.
  2. MinSTCut assigns inside/outside cell labels and marks separating facets.
  3. SWIFT displaces those facets towards the circle while holding the outer
     square fixed and checking its sampled mesh-quality budget.

  This sequence is also used in the Kelvin ball application. Here the analytic
  target isolates geometry fitting from PDE solves, level-set transport and
  field transfer. The @ref examples-mmg-mesh_adaptation "size-field tutorial"
  supplies the remeshing background; @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT explains the fitting model.

  @section examples-swift-background-size Prepare the background

  The initial grid has 13 vertices per axis and 288 triangles. Let
  @f$h_0=1/12@f$ be its spacing. The target edge size is
  @f[
    s(x)=s_{\mathrm{far}}-
      (s_{\mathrm{far}}-s_{\mathrm{near}})
      \exp\!\left[-\frac{\phi(x)^2}{w^2}\right],
  @f]
  with @f$s_{\mathrm{near}}=0.035@f$,
  @f$s_{\mathrm{far}}=0.14@f$ and @f$w=0.10@f$ in unit-square coordinates.
  Thus the requested mesh is fine near the circle and coarser away from it.
  These are chosen mesh-design parameters. The exponential is the weight
  supplied by @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Loss.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp background-adapt

  MMG receives scalar nodal sizes, lower/upper size limits and a gradation
  parameter of @f$1.3@f$. The four box corners are marked before adaptation.
  The size field is scoped to that call. All subsequent spaces and solvers
  are constructed on the adapted topology.

  @section examples-swift-background-classification Label the phases

  On each adapted cell @f$K@f$, define the local scale
  @f$h_K=\sqrt{|K|}@f$ and approximate the cell average
  @f[
    m_K=\frac{1}{|K|}\int_K
       \tanh\!\left(\frac{\phi(x)}{1.25h_K}\right)\,dx.
  @f]
  MinSTCut combines fidelity to these averages with a separating-facet cost.
  The facet weight is @f$0.01\min(h_{K^-},h_{K^+})@f$ on an interior facet.
  Second-order quadrature approximates the nonpolynomial phase average.
  The phase width and smoothing coefficient are classification choices.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp background-classify

  Cells receive attributes 1 and 2, separating edges receive attribute 10,
  and exterior edges receive attribute 20. Classification changes labels.
  The resulting polygonal interface follows existing mesh edges.

  @section examples-swift-background-fit Fit the marked interface

  The local @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT::Adapt workflow owns a vector-valued
  continuous @f$P_1@f$ displacement space. Its increment is zero on the outer
  square. The analytic distance and gradient supply the target throughout the
  solve.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp background-target
  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp background-fit

  Here `model.h` is fixed at the requested near-interface size, @f$0.035@f$.
  It scales the fitting model throughout this solve. An explicit geometric
  tolerance of @f$2.5\times10^{-3}@f$ replaces the automatic stopping target.
  The local SparseLU backend is selected explicitly. Other model and work-budget
  settings retain their defaults.

  `execute()` applies the accepted displacement to the mesh when the sampled
  quality budget is satisfied. A quality-valid best-effort result may still
  miss the geometric tolerance; this tutorial additionally requires
  `geometricTargetReached` before returning success. The fitting stage preserves
  the adapted connectivity and cell labels.

  @section examples-swift-background-result Inspect the result

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock

  @image html SWIFTBackgroundFitting-uniform.png "Uniform background."
  @image html SWIFTBackgroundFitting-classified.png "Adapted background with inside cells in cyan."
  @image html SWIFTBackgroundFitting-fitted.png "Fitted interface with the exact circle in navy."

  @endparblock

  These are the actual meshes written by the program. Edges remain visible to
  show where fitting compresses elements. The navy circle supplies the same
  analytic reference in every panel.

  The checked local run produced the following measurements:

  | Quantity | Result |
  | :--- | ---: |
  | Uniform background | 169 vertices, 288 triangles |
  | Adapted background | 288 vertices, 540 triangles |
  | Classified interface | 49 edges |
  | Accepted fitting steps | 5 |
  | Maximum radial error on the complete polygonal interface | @f$2.45685\times10^{-3}@f$ |
  | Minimum relative Jacobian | @f$0.05149@f$ |
  | Maximum relative distortion | @f$8.97282@f$ |
  | Outer-boundary displacement | @f$0@f$ |

  For a straight interface segment, the radial distance is largest at an
  endpoint and smallest at the orthogonal projection of the center, clamped
  to that segment. Evaluating these locations gives an independent maximum
  radial error on every complete segment. It agrees with the solver's
  sampled maximum to floating-point precision in this run. This checks the
  distance from the reconstructed interface to the circle; a two-sided
  Hausdorff calculation would also examine distances from the circle back
  to the polygon.

  Every triangle has a positive signed Jacobian, the square retains unit
  area, and fitting leaves its vertex and cell counts unchanged. The relative
  Jacobian and distortion values also show substantial local compression.
  Passing the configured budget permits this deformation. A subsequent PDE
  application should assess element conditioning and its own approximation
  error before choosing these mesh parameters.

  @section examples-swift-background-run Build, run and visualize

  Build and execute the `SWIFT_BackgroundFitting` target in a scratch directory.

  @code{.sh}
  cmake --build build --target SWIFT_BackgroundFitting
  mkdir -p results/background-fitting
  cd results/background-fitting
  ../../build/examples/Adaptation/SWIFT/SWIFT_BackgroundFitting
  @endcode

  `BackgroundFitting.xdmf` contains the named blocks `uniform`, `classified`
  and `fitted`. Keep its HDF5 companions alongside it. In ParaView, use
  <strong>Extract Block</strong> to select a stage, then <strong>Surface With Edges</strong>. On the last
  two blocks, threshold the cell array `Attribute` at 1 to show the disk.
  `BackgroundFitting.csv` records accepted-step diagnostics; the final monitor
  notification may repeat the last accepted step.

  @section examples-swift-background-full Complete program

  The complete program is available in @ref Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp "BackgroundFitting.cpp".

  @section examples-swift-background-next Continue

  The shared `SWIFT_ReconstructionP1`, `SWIFT_ReconstructionP2` and
  `SWIFT_ReconstructionP3` examples extend fitting to lobed targets and curved
  geometry. They retain an unchanged reference mesh while evaluating the
  displacement at all geometry nodes. See @ref guides-swift for ownership,
  higher-order geometry updates and diagnostic definitions.

  @example Adaptation/SWIFT/BackgroundFitting.cpp
*/
